From 12577b3ca807e8b0351a83ea7d84b3ca0b1ff06b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Justin Restivo <justin@restivo.me>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 14:12:29 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] Stack protector (verified):
 -fstack-protector[-strong|-all]

Port of the stack-canary pass of Chamois-CompCert (D. Monniaux,
"Memory simulations, security and optimization in a verified
compiler", CPP 2024) to AbsInt master.  backend/Canary.v and
backend/Canaryproof.v come from Chamois (2023-09, before its
memory-model changes), adapted to upstream:

- RTL: no trapping mode on Iload, no branch-prediction field on Icond,
  argument typing premise on internal calls.
- Mem.loadv/storev now check that the access fits below the modulus;
  canary_needed therefore requires canary_offset + canary_size <=
  max_unsigned (Chamois only bounded the offset), and the proof shows
  the canary load/store passes the check (canary_load_bound).

The pass runs on RTL after dead code elimination.  For every function
with stack-allocated variables (all functions with -all), it reserves
an aligned slot after the variables, stores the canary there on entry,
and before every return and tail call compares it with the canary and
calls __stack_chk_fail on mismatch.  The proof is a forward simulation
in which the check never fails for programs without undefined
behaviour; it does not prove that canaries stop attacks.  The only new
assumptions are the canary values (Values.canary32_val/canary64_val)
and the two option flags.

Targets: x86-64 and IA32 (canary at %fs:40 / %gs:20) and AArch64
(__stack_chk_guard), Linux only.  ARM, PowerPC and RISC-V define
getcanary := None, so the pass is the identity there.
